::ENG2_U06_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which oral outline order is strongest?{
=A. Opening, claim, evidence, reasoning, closing.#Correct. This order gives listeners a clear path.
~B. Closing, evidence, random fact, opening, claim.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
~C. Evidence only with no claim.#Incorrect. Evidence needs a claim and reasoning.
~D. Random facts with no closing.#Incorrect. This is not organized oral communication.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] What is a claim?{
~A. A source title only.#Incorrect. A title is not a claim.
=B. The main point the speaker wants the audience to accept or understand.#Correct. A claim states the speaker's main point.
~C. A random number from a survey.#Incorrect. A number may be evidence, not the claim.
~D. A closing thank-you only.#Incorrect. That may end a presentation, but it is not a claim.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which evidence is most credible?{
~A. Someone said it is true.#Incorrect. This is vague and unsupported.
~B. I feel like students wait around.#Incorrect. This is personal opinion.
=C. A survey of 120 students reports that 68 remain on campus after dismissal at least one day each week.#Correct. This is specific source-based evidence.
~D. Everybody knows the answer.#Incorrect. This is not credible evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which sentence gives reasoning?{
~A. Source A surveyed students.#Incorrect. This is source information, not reasoning.
~B. Source B is from a handbook.#Incorrect. This identifies a source.
~C. The survey number is 120.#Incorrect. This is evidence detail.
=D. This matters because students who already remain on campus need a safer and more productive supervised location.#Correct. This explains why the evidence supports the claim.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] What central idea connects Source A and Source B?{
=A. Students remain on campus after dismissal, and supervised locations matter.#Correct. This idea reflects both the survey and handbook.
~B. Students should block hallways.#Incorrect. The handbook says students should not block hallways.
~C. Surveys are not useful.#Incorrect. Source A provides useful survey information.
~D. No students stay after school.#Incorrect. Source A says many students remain on campus.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which opening best prepares listeners for the topic?{
~A. I have random facts.#Incorrect. This is vague and weak.
=B. Today I will explain why a supervised after-school study space would help students who remain on campus.#Correct. This opening identifies the topic and purpose.
~C. This is about stuff after school.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. I do not know what this is about.#Incorrect. This does not prepare listeners.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Why is the handbook excerpt useful evidence?{
~A. It is unrelated to school procedures.#Incorrect. It is directly related to school procedures.
~B. It is only a personal opinion.#Incorrect. A handbook excerpt is an official school source.
=C. It states an expectation that students should wait in supervised locations.#Correct. This directly supports the need for a supervised space.
~D. It proves students never wait after school.#Incorrect. It does not say that.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which pair correctly labels evidence and reasoning?{
~A. Evidence: This matters because students need safety. Reasoning: 49 students wait in hallways.#Incorrect. The labels are reversed.
~B. Evidence: I think it is good. Reasoning: survey.#Incorrect. This is vague and mislabeled.
~C. Evidence: random thoughts. Reasoning: source name.#Incorrect. This is not accurate.
=D. Evidence: 49 surveyed students wait in hallways or similar areas. Reasoning: This shows a supervised study space would address an existing need.#Correct. The evidence gives source information, and reasoning explains it.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which closing statement is strongest?{
=A. A supervised study space would turn after-school waiting time into safer and more productive academic time.#Correct. This reinforces the claim and reasoning.
~B. That is it.#Incorrect. This is weak and undeveloped.
~C. I am done talking.#Incorrect. This does not reinforce the presentation.
~D. The survey was a survey.#Incorrect. This is repetitive and weak.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Which statement best identifies a central idea?{
~A. The source has numbers.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=B. Supervised after-school locations matter because many students remain on campus after dismissal.#Correct. This is a complete central idea.
~C. The handbook is a book.#Incorrect. This is not the central idea.
~D. A bus loop exists.#Incorrect. This detail is not the central idea.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which evidence should a speaker avoid using as the main support?{
~A. Student survey data with a stated sample size.#Incorrect. This is credible evidence.
~B. An official school handbook excerpt.#Incorrect. This is credible evidence.
=C. A vague statement that "everybody says so."#Correct. This is not credible or specific.
~D. A specific number from a relevant survey.#Incorrect. This can be credible evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which transition would best introduce evidence?{
~A. In conclusion#Incorrect. This signals a closing, not evidence.
~B. Anyway#Incorrect. This is informal and weak.
~C. Randomly#Incorrect. This weakens organization.
=D. According to Source A#Correct. This clearly introduces source-based evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which sentence combines claim and reason clearly?{
=A. The school should create a supervised study space because many students remain on campus after dismissal.#Correct. This states a claim and a reason.
~B. Study space, students, after school, survey.#Incorrect. This is not a complete claim.
~C. The handbook is there.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. I want a room.#Incorrect. This is personal and lacks reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What makes oral organization different from simply listing facts?{
~A. Oral organization avoids all evidence.#Incorrect. Strong organization uses evidence.
=B. Oral organization connects facts in a sequence that listeners can follow.#Correct. Organization helps listeners understand how ideas connect.
~C. Oral organization means speaking as fast as possible.#Incorrect. Speed does not create organization.
~D. Oral organization means using no closing.#Incorrect. A closing helps listeners remember the point.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which detail from Source A best supports the claim that many students remain on campus?{
~A. The source is called Student Survey Summary.#Incorrect. The title does not prove the number of students.
~B. Some students have opinions.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. 68 of 120 surveyed students reported remaining on campus at least one day each week after dismissal.#Correct. This detail directly supports the claim.
~D. The handbook has rules.#Incorrect. This is from Source B and does not give the survey count.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which reasoning sentence best explains Source B?{
~A. The handbook exists.#Incorrect. This does not explain why the evidence matters.
~B. The handbook has pages.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant.
~C. Source B is a source.#Incorrect. This is circular and not reasoning.
=D. Because the handbook expects supervised waiting locations, a study space would help students follow school procedures.#Correct. This explains how the evidence supports the claim.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaking note is clearest?{
=A. Claim: The school should provide a supervised after-school study space.#Correct. This note is clear and labeled.
~B. Stuff: after school maybe.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. Say something about halls.#Incorrect. This is not a clear speaking note.
~D. Talk random source.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Which source detail develops the central idea that supervision matters?{
~A. The survey includes 120 students.#Incorrect. This supports student presence more than supervision.
=B. The handbook says students should wait in supervised locations.#Correct. This directly develops the idea that supervision matters.
~C. Students completed a survey.#Incorrect. This does not focus on supervision.
~D. The bus loop exists.#Incorrect. This does not develop the supervision idea.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which problem would hurt an oral presentation most?{
~A. Using a clear opening.#Incorrect. A clear opening helps.
~B. Explaining evidence.#Incorrect. Explanation helps.
=C. Jumping from claim to unrelated facts with no reasoning.#Correct. This would make the message hard to follow.
~D. Ending with a closing statement.#Incorrect. A closing helps listeners.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which item is evidence, not reasoning?{
~A. This matters because students need safe spaces.#Incorrect. This explains importance, so it is reasoning.
~B. Therefore, the claim is stronger.#Incorrect. This is reasoning language.
~C. This shows the need is real.#Incorrect. This is reasoning.
=D. Source A reports that 49 students usually wait in hallways or similar areas.#Correct. This is a source-based fact, so it is evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which checklist best matches Lesson 2?{
=A. Claim, credible evidence, reasoning, logical order, closing.#Correct. This is the Lesson 2 organization process.
~B. Random facts, no claim, no order, no closing.#Incorrect. This weakens oral communication.
~C. Opinion only, no evidence, no explanation.#Incorrect. Credible evidence is required.
~D. Closing first, then unrelated source details.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Why is a specific survey number stronger than "many people say"?{
~A. Specific numbers are always fiction.#Incorrect. The survey number is source-based.
=B. Specific numbers give concrete evidence that listeners can evaluate.#Correct. Concrete evidence is stronger than vague claims.
~C. Vague wording is always more credible.#Incorrect. Vague wording is weaker.
~D. Evidence should never be specific.#Incorrect. Specific evidence is usually stronger.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Which sentence best summarizes Source A?{
~A. No students remain on campus.#Incorrect. Source A says many students remain on campus.
~B. All students leave immediately.#Incorrect. This contradicts Source A.
=C. A student survey found that many students remain on campus after dismissal, and many wait in informal areas.#Correct. This accurately summarizes Source A.
~D. The handbook lists supervised locations.#Incorrect. That describes Source B.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which oral note best introduces reasoning?{
~A. Source A says 49 students wait in hallways.#Incorrect. This introduces evidence.
~B. The survey counted students.#Incorrect. This is evidence.
~C. The handbook has a rule.#Incorrect. This is evidence.
=D. This evidence matters because it shows the school already has students who need an organized waiting location.#Correct. This introduces reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which final statement best connects claim, evidence, and reasoning?{
=A. Since survey data and handbook expectations both show a need for supervised locations, a study space would support student safety and learning after school.#Correct. This connects sources, claim, and reasoning.
~B. The source set is over now.#Incorrect. This does not connect the ideas.
~C. Students and handbooks are things.#Incorrect. This is vague and not logical.
~D. I listed facts, so no explanation is needed.#Incorrect. Evidence needs reasoning.
}
